{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788304290,"nonce":"bGLM9cxLUekRuxpMD67yAlf8Aug=","message":"DOWNGRADED p1 -\u003e p3, AND THE DEAD-MAN SWITCH IS NOT BEING BUILT. Evan challenged the premise on 2026-09-01 — \"is this really a problem? the game will trigger the agent eventually, or it's broken\" — and the challenge holds. Recording the analysis rather than the conclusion, because the conclusion depends on two things that shipped the same day and could be undone.\n\n## The fact that settles it: AN ADVANCE ENDS PAUSED\n\n`TimeDriver` publishes `paused_on_exit` on every advance, and its own comment calls it \"the turn-based contract, stated every time rather than only when it fails. **False here means the colony is still running.**\" Confirmed three independent ways: the comment, `accept/61794cd-bleed-triage.py` check 7.6c passing live (\"whatever happened, the game is left PAUSED\"), and the orchestrator's own manual reads, which came back `paused: true, speed: Paused` after every advance this session.\n\nSo a dead client does not, by itself, leave the game running. The advance completes on the mod's side — which needs no client — and then pauses.\n\n## Which means this issue's own evidence was a symptom of `1113019`\n\nThe body reports \"~60,000 ticks — a full in-game day — elapsed unobserved, and it happened more than once.\" For the game to still be at `Ultrafast` when a human eventually typed `rwa pause`, **the advance must never have ended.** An advance that ends, pauses.\n\n`1113019` (filed and ruled today) is exactly that: an `until` advance whose predicate is already true at arm time has no false-\u003etrue edge left, and with no `timeout_ticks` it is unbounded. One was measured this session running **187,541 ticks** and it was stopped only by `722c951`'s casualty halt. A full in-game day of unobserved ticking is precisely what that bug produces before anyone notices.\n\nThat is not proof the M1 incidents were `1113019` — the transcript directories `136-advance` and `187-advance` are empty, which is the client-side write-order defect this issue's body describes, so the arguments in flight cannot be recovered. But it is a mechanism that fully explains the observation, and it is now fixed.\n\n## The exposure after today\n\nTwo changes ruled today bound it from both ends:\n\n- **`1113019`** defaults an unbounded `until` advance to 60,000 ticks (one in-game day). So the worst case for a client that dies at the first tick of an advance is one day, and then the game pauses.\n- **`280fb78`** makes every letter and every `alert_on` halt the advance unconditionally, alongside the existing casualty, dialog and red-error halts. So in practice an advance ends far sooner than its bound whenever anything happens at all.\n\nAnd Evan's own framing of the 60,000 default is that a quiet day is the NORMAL idle unit — \"a full day without doing anything while you're fully set is pretty typical.\" An unattended day that turns out to have been unsupervised is therefore a day-shaped loss, not an open-ended one.\n\n## What is genuinely left, and why a watchdog would not catch it\n\nThe residual is the mod's own completion path failing to run: a crash, or an unhandled exception inside the advance. That is Evan's \"or it's broken\", and a dead-man switch **living inside the same mod** does not survive the case that breaks the mod. Catching it needs supervision outside the process, which is a different issue and not this one.\n\n## What is still worth doing, cheaply\n\nThe **client-side** half named in this issue's body is unaffected and still worth fixing on its own branch: `rwa` mkdirs the step directory before sending and writes `cmd.json`/`result.json` only after the result returns, so a client death leaves an empty directory and the transcript cannot say what was in flight. That is why this analysis has to reason from mechanism rather than from the M1 record. Making the death legible costs little and pays every time something goes wrong.\n\n## Reopen this if\n\n- an advance is ever observed NOT paused on exit under a live mod, or\n- `1113019`'s default bound is removed or raised without bound, or\n- M1's re-run shows game time elapsing that no advance accounts for.\n\nUntil one of those, building a heartbeat, choosing a threshold and defending it against false positives is work spent on a failure mode that two other fixes already close.","files":null}]}